URL-based rules protect at the HTTP layer, but method-level security lets you secure individual service or controller methods with fine-grained expressions. This is invaluable when multiple URLs share a service method, or when authorization depends on business logic (e.g., a user can only edit their own resources).

Enable method security by adding @EnableMethodSecurity to your configuration class. This activates support for @PreAuthorize, @PostAuthorize, @PreFilter, and @PostFilter annotations. The older @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity with @Secured and @RolesAllowed still works but is deprecated — prefer @EnableMethodSecurity.

@PreAuthorize runs before the method executes. If the expression returns false, an AccessDeniedException is thrown and the method is never called. @PostAuthorize runs after the method returns, giving you access to the return value via the returnObject variable — useful for checking ownership (e.g., ensure the returned order belongs to the requesting user).

@Secured and @RolesAllowed are simpler, annotation-based alternatives that only support role names, not full SpEL expressions. Use @PreAuthorize for anything complex. A key advantage of method security over URL rules: it works even for service methods called internally, not just from HTTP controllers.

